  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nHigh level clouds from the approaching Clipper low continue to move \ninto the U.P. early this morning, with showers and storms \npotentially moving into the Ironwood area by the late morning hours \nas the Clipper's warm front starts to arrive. While the GFS shows \nmodest isentropic lift along the warm front and PWATs increasing to \n1.25 to 1.50 inches, given the limited amount of instability within \nthe atmosphere (only 1 kJ/kg or less) and sub-optimal 0-6 km shear \n(~30 knots), thinking that while a strong storm or two could be seen \nover the far west (generally late this afternoon to early this \nevening), no storms should technically reach severe weather criteria \n(i.e. tornadoes, hail of 1 inch or greater, 60+ mph winds). That \nbeing said, wouldn't be surprised if we had some storms pop-up and \nbring some stronger winds up to 50 mph and small hail up to a dime \nor nickel in size down to the sfc. In addition to these hazards, \nheavy rainfall could be seen in the stronger showers and storms, \nwith some spots potentially receiving over an inch of rainfall, even \nin the convection that occurs tonight through Wednesday as the cold \nfront followed by the wrap-around moisture behind the Clipper moves \nover the rest of Upper MI. While there is still some uncertainty on \nthe exact timing of the warm front, cold front, and wrap-around \nmoisture of the low, and whether the front's occlude or not, there \nis high confidence (60-90%) that rainfall will be seen across the \narea between now and Thursday morning. However, given the convective \nnature of the rainfall, some spots could receive a 'feast' of \nrainfall while others see a 'famine'; the latest NBM probabilities \nshow a general 10-60% chance for receiving more than an inch of \nrainfall between this morning and Thursday morning, with the highest \nchances being seen in the east; that being said, the latest NBM also \nshows up to around a 20% chance for less than a tenth of an inch in \nthe areas near the WI border, particularly in the south central, for \nthe same time period. Expect the rainfall over the U.P. to wrap up \nWednesday night as the (probably) lake-enhanced rain showers dwindle \ndown across the area. In addition to the rainfall, the Clipper is \nexpected to bring strong southerly winds to the Great Lakes. This in \nturn is projected to create dangerous rip currents and high waves \nalong the beaches of Schoolcraft County on Wednesday. Therefore, \nwhile one has not been issued yet, expect a Beach Hazard Statement \nto be forthcoming this afternoon for Schoolcraft County on Wednesday.\n\nExpect fairly normal temperatures this week into next week across \nthe area as dry weather returns via incoming high pressure ridging \nonce again late this week. While it looks like we may get a day or \ntwo of reprieve from rainfall, medium range guidance does suggest \nthat additional shortwaves enter the Upper Midwest sometime this \nweekend through early next week as ridging deamplifies over the \nCentral CONUS and troughing develops over the western U.S.",
